      "completed": true,
      "answer": "**Answer:** RFC 2616 (HTTP/1.1, June 1999) is **not current**. The IETF Datatracker lists it as \"Obsoleted by RFC 7230, RFC 7231, RFC 7232, RFC 7233, RFC 7234, RFC 7235\" (June 2014), and those in turn were obsoleted by **RFC 9110 (HTTP Semantics)** and **RFC 9111 (HTTP Caching)** in June 2022. Verified verbatim from the fetched HTTP Working Group passage: \"These RFCs collectively obsolete all preceding RFCs defining HTTP, including RFC 1945, RFC 2068, RFC 2616, RFC 2617, RFC 7230-5, and RFC 7540.\"\n\n**Sources:**\n-  (IETF Datatracker status page for RFC 2616)\n-  (HTTP Working Group — RFC 9110/9111 obsoletion statement)\n-  and  (current HTTP standards, June 2022)\n\nFINAL LABEL: supported\n\nSources:\n- https://datatracker.ietf.org/doc/rfc2616\n- https://httpwg.org/specs\n- https://www.rfc-editor.org/rfc/rfc9111.html",
